1 | INTRODUCTION
Collaborative statisticians are integrated into academic research in a number of different ways. Many statistics and biostatistics departments
within universities have found success creating a “consulting hub” that offers broad services to those requesting them. Some faculty within those
departments also grow to be subject matter experts in other domains through long-term collaborations with outside researchers. There are also
other collaborative statisticians who operate entirely outside of mathematical or statistical departments but who are still within the broader aca
demic structure. Within the group of collaborative statisticians, there exist isolated statisticians (Figure 1), those outside structured statistics
related departments or statistical consulting hubs. Isolated statisticians face many challenges in terms of professional support and networking,
mentorship and clear role expectations (Mehta et al., 2022). It is worthwhile to note that “isolated” is a relative term. A small consulting unit with
one master's level analyst and one Ph.D. level statistician could easily be argued to contain two isolated statisticians.
Embedded statisticians (Figure 1) are isolated statisticians who are employed by other domain-specific departments where there is a suffi
cient volume of research being performed to justify an in-house statistician or analyst. In academic health centres, 18% of statisticians reported
primary appointments in clinical departments rather than biostatistics or statistics/data science departments (Hanlon et al., 2022). There are both
advantages and disadvantages for statisticians to be embedded in a clinical department (Spratt et al., 2017). One key advantage is the close inter
actions and long-term collaborations that can develop between embedded statisticians and clinical domain experts (Spratt et al., 2017). From
these collaborations, embedded statisticians can find tremendous professional fulfilment, with projects related to improving patient care and clini
cal outcomes (Spratt et al., 2017).
However, embedded statisticians encounter the same challenges of isolated statisticians regarding professional support and networking, men
torship and clear role expectations (Mehta et al., 2022). Moreover, embedded statisticians are burdened by the requirement to communicate their
needs, expectations and values to supervisors who are not statisticians and sometimes are also not researchers, within the structure and policies
of their home clinical department; departmental-specific policies and structure can have a significant influence upon embedded statisticians' suc
cess and promotion (Spratt et al., 2017). These burdens are experienced by faculty and staff at every degree level. The metrics that have been pre
viously proposed for collaborative statisticians often assume that the same language is being spoken between the statistician and supervisor
(Griffith et al., 2022; Hanlon et al., 2022; Rubio et al., 2011) and may not encapsulate the activities performed by an embedded statistician. Thus,
there is a knowledge gap regarding metrics tailored to meet the needs of the embedded statistician and clinical supervisor. In this paper, we will
review the contributions of collaborative statistics, and embedded statisticians in particular, to the entire research enterprise; discuss perception
of value by statisticians; outline metrics needed for embedded statisticians to communicate their value; and finally, provide examples on how
embedded statistics can create value reports to share with their supervisors. Specifically, we will explore (1) the critical role of collaborative statis
ticians and the benefits and challenges of the embedded statistician model, (2) the need for additional metrics specific to embedded statisticians
which measure value and (3) how to design a value report. We will conclude with thoughts on future directions for development of metrics and
reporting systems for statisticians embedded in clinical departments.
