2 | CRITICAL ROLE OF COLLABORATIVE STATISTICIANS AND BENEFITS AND CHALLENGES OF THE EMBEDDED STATISTICIAN MODEL
For those trained in statistics and data science, there is an appreciation for the roles that statisticians play in every step in the entire investigative
cycle from stating the research question, to trial design, data cleaning, performing the analyses and final interpretations of the results, all of which
benefit from a trained analyst (Wild & Pfannkuch, 1999). These diverse roles necessitate that collaborative statisticians perform activities that
cover a broad spectrum, with these activities varying across level of need and complexity of projects (Griffith et al., 2022). In clinical departments,
the amount of research training and experience that clinicians have can vary from fairly modest to rather extensive and can depend on the spe
cialty. An advantage for clinical departments with embedded statisticians is being able to have statistical input early and throughout projects. This
early and in-depth involvement in projects can improve rigour, reproducibility and data integrity. Furthermore, embedded statisticians gain in
depth knowledge of their domain-specific field and can bridge gaps between clinical and statistical perspectives (Mehta et al., 2022). Greater con
tent knowledge then allows embedded statisticians to not only construct more informed statistical models but also interpret results and better
communicate findings to clinical audiences. Unfortunately, in other domains, there is the persistent idea that collaborative statisticians are simply
data analysts and that the job begins after the data have been collected and ends when a p-value is delivered. When this is the case, how does
one show that the large spans of time spent on tasks that seem ancillary are not only necessary but valuable?
3 | EXISTING MODELS FOR VALUE METRICS AND DOCUMENTATION
Previous publications have outlined guidelines and metrics to best document the work and value of collaborative statisticians (Griffith et al., 2022;
Hanlon et al., 2022; Mazumdar et al., 2015; Rubio et al., 2011; Sharp et al., 2016; Spratt et al., 2017). A key component of these guidelines is the
emphasis on the value of contributions by collaborative statisticians across multiple domains. While collaborative statisticians most recognizably
contribute to the analysis and application of statistical methods, they also make substantial and impactful contributions to study design and imple
mentation, manuscript/presentation/grant preparation, teaching and mentoring and service. Another critical component introduced in the Rubio
et al. (2011) and Mazumdar et al. (2015) papers is the estimation of degree of involvement/effort related to activities. Rubio et al. (2011) advo
cated for the use of an ordinal metric (small, medium and large) that relates to the hours of effort required for a project (<10, 10–50 and 50+ h,
respectively). In the Mazumdar et al. (2015) paper, they developed a framework to assess the scientific contributions of the collaborative biostatis
ticians, classifying contributions as minor, medium or major; their paper gives example of activities that would align with these different classifica
tions. This type of effort appraisal will not only give better context to the contributions of embedded statisticians but would also give their clinical
supervisors and administrators a metric to evaluate workload and effort distribution.
4 | METRICS FOR EMBEDDED STATISTICIANS
While many metrics for collaborative statisticians apply to embedded statisticians, statisticians embedded in clinical departments also have unique
evaluation needs. Spratt et al. (2017) noted that the working environment in clinical departments differs from that of a statistics or biostatistics
department in that patient care is, rightly so, the priority. Overall, the ability of embedded statisticians to contribute to scholarly activity is highly
dependent on the schedules and priorities of their clinical collaborators, meaning that scholarly productivity for embedded statisticians can be more
affected by opportunity than their skills as a collaborative statistician. The primacy of patient care can slow the research process and, in turn, the
rate of publications for a collaborative statistician. Furthermore, the emphasis on writing and attaining grant funding for clinical faculty is lower,
especially if they themselves are not on a tenure track. However, the focus on patient care also provides an opportunity for embedded statisticians
to demonstrate their value by showing how their contributions to clinical projects have improved health outcomes and hospital processes.
Additionally, teaching opportunities and responsibilities for statisticians embedded in clinical departments can vary and be irregular. In clinical
departments, there is more emphasis on one-on-one interactions compared with teaching in more formal courses (Spratt et al., 2017). Likewise,
many clinical departments do not have affiliated graduate degree programmes, with mentoring activities focusing on medical students, residents,
fellows and other faculty (Spratt et al., 2017). If more formal teaching and graduate committee work is desired by an embedded statistician, they
will need to seek out appointments in other departments and colleges, as well as communicate and negotiate this effort in their home clinical
department.
Furthermore, many metrics that are underemphasized and prioritized in other fields can be of great importance to statisticians embedded in
clinical departments. In a survey of leaders of collaborative biostatistics units in academic health centres, Hanlon et al. (2022) found that metrics
appraised as the most important in terms of career advancement were coinvestigator status on NIH or similar grants as well as first or second
authored peer-reviewed publications, while middle authorship was ranked as one of the least important factors. However, collaborative statisti
cians, especially those embedded in clinical departments, often have increased middle authorship papers, with less emphasis on first authorship.
