Additionally, co-authorship as a collaborative biostatistician typically still represents a substantial contribution to publications (Sharp et al., 2016).
This is why documenting degree of effort, as mentioned above, is extremely important. Moreover, for projects with planned extensive statistical
work or modelling, collaborative statisticians should negotiate and advocate for a higher authorship listing.
Additionally, collaborative biostatisticians—and embedded statisticians in particular—tend not to have their own research programme in the tra
ditional sense but instead are more likely to have a statistical “theme,” that is, a set of specialty skills and tools that they can apply to their domain
specific fields. Due to their expertise and vital skills, embedded statisticians often are involved on many projects at once, linked not by the research
topic but more by the specific statistical needs contributed by the statistician. Furthermore, being involved in multiple projects, and with multiple
collaborators, should be viewed as a positive for a collaborative statistician, supporting their well-rounded abilities as well as their reputation as a
collaborator (Griffith et al., 2022). Table 1 provides a framework to highlight which of the metrics previously discussed are most useful to embedded
statisticians as well as suggest additional metrics to be considered, with metrics included with appreciation for both clinical department relevance
and ease of data capture. There are four domains in the metric framework: (1) collaboration, (2) research output/productivity, (3) mentoring and
(4) education. Metrics that are particularly specific to embedded statisticians and that are not routinely captured include time reporting for both pro
ject initiation to completion/outcome and initial statistical consultation to statistical outcome completion. These variables will account for the often
longer timelines needed to publish with clinical collaborators. Additionally, the authors strongly emphasize the need to report not only author order
of manuscripts and presentation but also level/context of contribution. Level of contribution can vary widely and may include supervising trainees'
statistical analyses, writing the majority of the methods and results sections, leading the study design plan from onset and/or being an integral
coinvestigator on related grant funding for project. We encourage embedded statisticians to develop a list with descriptors of levels of contribution
that occur most frequently in their collaborations so that integrating these metrics into reports can be streamlined.
