5 | DESIGNING A VALUE REPORT
While defining metrics for embedded statisticians is important, it is just as important for embedded statisticians to be able to develop reports to
best communicate theory work and value with their nonstatistician supervisors. Unfortunately, there is not a standardized way to generate
reports to communicate one's value. These authors emphasize that embedded statisticians need to take responsibility for these reports and con
trol their own narrative. Reports or work summaries generated by departmental administration can lack description of both the breadth and depth
of activities performed by embedded statisticians, often focusing on small snapshots of their work (e.g., number of publications and external
funding). There are several important factors for embedded statisticians to consider when tracking metrics to develop their report:
1. Content: What metrics? (see above)
2. Process: How do we document research workflow and involvement at each stage?
3. Software: What software should we use to track metrics and easily generate reports?
4. System: How do we organize and classify the metrics to best reflect our activities?
It is both necessary and beneficial for embedded statisticians to meet and discuss what information is useful to their supervisors, because the
approaches to the above will vary depending on the department as well as on the individual statistician. Figure 2 presents a sample value report for
an embedded statistician in a clinical department that was created in consultation with the clinical departmental chair. Within the authors' clinical
department, we are piloting the use of these reports for quarterly productivity updates, which has spurred increased communication between
embedded statisticians and not only the department chair but also with other departmental leaders (i.e., divisions chiefs and senior administrators).
These reports also augment existing quarterly research reports from the finance office on grant funding for faculty by highlighting information not
captured by institutional financial reporting, particularly grants in which a faculty member is a coinvestigator or other personnel and not the principal
investigator. Utilizing visualizations helps create easy to understand value reports that focus on maximizing communication and understanding.
However, while it is important for embedded statisticians to generate these reports, the system needs to be streamlined with minimal time
and effort to be maintained by the statistician. This will ensure that the reporting system is sustainable and does not contribute to burnout. Again,
this is why close communication with supervisors is necessary to align expectations. One way to streamline report generation is to align and auto
mate components when possible. This can include having set time periods for reporting (e.g., quarterly), utilizing existing institutional infrastruc
ture to obtain needed information (e.g., grants offices) and taking advantage of existing features of websites to gain information (e.g., PubMed
alerts). Furthermore, many available task and project management software programs have customizable automation to easily classify activities
and track time spent on projects. A more advanced step would be to develop specialized, interactive tools to track and summarize metrics. An
institutional case study (Schmidt et al., 2017) demonstrated that a web-based dashboard application for research project tracking in an epidemiol
ogy/biostatistics department, using REDCap and R programming, reduced study intake time, increased efficiency and improved workload alloca
tion. Similar integrations should be used to develop dashboards to track metrics and research workflows to better—and more efficiently
summarize activities and generate value reports for embedded statisticians.
