6 | FUTURE DIRECTIONS
The next step for development of metrics and reporting systems for statisticians embedded in clinical departments is additional study of value
perceptions. Thus far, to our knowledge, the population of embedded statisticians, their clinical collaborators and their clinical supervisors has not
been studied. Recent surveys of collaborative statisticians have evaluated perceptions on value and recognition by colleagues, departments and
institutions, with these studies comparing collaborative statisticians with and without statistician/methodologist supervisors. Sharp et al. (2016)
conducted a survey of statistical consultants in university settings to assess perceived value. Twenty-seven respondents were within established
consulting centres while 12 were not. Statisticians not in consulting centres did not have significant differences in rates of agreement, compared
with those in consulting centres, in terms of being valued by the institution (50% vs. 67%), by faculty outside department (83% vs. 96%) and by
faculty in their department (58% vs. 74%), though these observed rates were slightly lower. There were also similar agreement rates reported
between statisticians not in a consulting centre versus those within viewing that their work contributed to promotion (58% vs. 56%) and publica
tion requirements (67% vs 69%). In another survey focussed on collaborative biostatisticians in academic health centres, the majority (87%) of
respondents thought that their work was valued by their collaborators (Devick et al., 2022). Encouragingly, this rate was similar for statisticians
with (87%) and without (82%) supervisors who were methodologists (Devick et al., 2022).
However, these studies also noted differences for statisticians not in a consulting centre or collaborative subunit or those without a method
ology supervisor. Sharp et al. (2016) found that statisticians not in a consulting centre were more likely (83%) to report a higher consulting work
load relative to other responsibilities compared with statisticians in a consulting centre (63%). Additionally, statisticians not in a consulting centre
were less likely to agree that their work contributed to pay raises (42%) compared with those who were in consulting centres (93%). Devick et al.
also reported that statisticians without a methodologist supervisor, compared with those with one, were less likely to report the ability to collabo
rate with other statisticians or other researchers with quantitative backgrounds (33% vs. 62%) and the opportunity to collaborate specifically on
methodological research (15% vs. 39%).
The work of Sharp et al. and Devick et al. needs to be replicated and extended to the population of embedded statisticians, their clinical col
laborators and their clinical supervisors. Such work would include a survey of extant metrics and reporting systems used in clinical departments
and the value perceptions of clinical faculty and leadership. Importantly, research is needed on the economics of the embedded statistician model
to answer questions such as cost effectiveness and contribution of embedded statisticians to physician promotion, retention and well-being.
7 | CONCLUSION
We have provided here the context for understanding the unique challenges of embedded statisticians within the larger academic research para
digm and, in particular, the need for these individuals to advocate for themselves in a way that translates the full extent of their value to their
supervisors. We have also given examples of metrics that we have found useful in our own efforts to communicate with such individuals. We
hope this helps to guide a continued discussion on methods and practices for supporting isolated and embedded statisticians in this rapidly grow
ing field.
